###
title: The Taylor series of eˣ fails at x = −20
line: Terms reach $4.3 \times 10^{7}$ for an answer of $2.1 \times 10^{-9}$, so the sum is off by more than 10%; $1/e^{20}$ is good to $10^{-14}$.
claim: Summing the Taylor series of $e^{x}$ at $x = -20$ term by term in double precision gives a relative error above 0.1 (the largest term is about $4.3 \times 10^{7}$ and the answer about $2.1 \times 10^{-9}$), while $1/e^{20}$ by the same series has a relative error below $10^{-14}$.
topic: math
section: floating point
reference: $e^{-20} = 2.061153622438558 \times 10^{-9}$ from the libm exp (correctly rounded to within an ulp); Forsythe, Malcolm and Moler, Computer Methods for Mathematical Computations (1977), section 1.3, the textbook example.
scope: The series is summed left to right until the terms stop changing the sum, about 100 terms. The error depends on rounding in the largest terms, so its exact size is not claimed, only that it exceeds 10% here.
lane: ai
by: claude
cost: fast
###

N_TERMS: Int = 120
X_NEG = -20.0
EXP_MINUS_20 = 2.061153622438558e-9

#| exp(x) by its Taylor series, summed left to right; also the largest |term|.
def exp_series(x: Num) {
    s = 1.0
    term = 1.0
    big = 1.0
    for k from 1 to N_TERMS {
        term = term * x / k
        s = s + term
        big = max(big, abs(term))
    }
    return s, big
}

direct, largest_term = exp_series(X_NEG)
pos, unused = exp_series(-X_NEG)
rel_err_direct = abs(direct - EXP_MINUS_20) / EXP_MINUS_20
rel_err_reciprocal = abs(1.0 / pos - EXP_MINUS_20) / EXP_MINUS_20
exp_minus_20 = exp(X_NEG)
show exp_minus_20
show largest_term
show rel_err_direct
show rel_err_reciprocal

#: the figure: the size of each term at x = -20, the answer, and the rounding error the largest term carries
UNIT_ROUNDOFF = 1.1102230246251565e-16
fg_k = zeros(N_TERMS)
fg_term = zeros(N_TERMS)
fg_t = 1.0
for fg_i to N_TERMS {
    fg_t = fg_t * X_NEG / (fg_i + 1.0)
    fg_k[fg_i] = fg_i + 1.0
    fg_term[fg_i] = abs(fg_t)
}
fg_ends = [1.0, N_TERMS * 1.0]
figure("Terms of the Taylor series of exp(-20)", "term k", "size")
yscale("log")
plot(fg_k, fg_term, "|x^k / k!|")
style("|x^k / k!|", "s1 nomarker")
plot(fg_ends, [largest_term * UNIT_ROUNDOFF, largest_term * UNIT_ROUNDOFF], "rounding error of the largest term")
style("rounding error of the largest term", "dash s2 nomarker")
plot(fg_ends, [EXP_MINUS_20, EXP_MINUS_20], "the answer, exp(-20)")
style("the answer, exp(-20)", "dot s6 nomarker")

test "the largest term is about 4.3e7" {
    assert_near(largest_term, 4.3e7, 0.05e7)
}
test "the series at x = -20 is off by more than 10%" {
    assert_gt(rel_err_direct, 0.1)
}
test "1 / exp(20) by the same series is good to 1e-14" {
    assert_lt(rel_err_reciprocal, 1.0e-14)
}

